From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 +0000
From: NebulaAGI <gyccc@atomcode.com>
Date: Wed, 6 Aug 2025 04:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] BuilderTest.testChemistry: skip on remote-DTD
 ParsingException (rsc.org malformed entity)

`BuilderTest.testChemistry` parses a document whose DOCTYPE references
the remote DTD `http://www.rsc.org/dtds/rscart37.dtd`, which in turn
pulls the entity `http://www.rsc.org/dtds/entities/rsc_x.ent`.

Upstream wraps `builder.build(s)` in `catch (IOException ex)` and
skips when `CITestUtil.shouldIgnore(ex)` fires.  This works when the
remote host is unreachable / returns a 4xx-5xx that Xerces surfaces
as an `IOException`.

It does **not** work when the remote host **returns a malformed
entity page** (e.g. an HTML rate-limit / error page instead of a
well-formed DTD entity declaration).  In that case Xerces raises a
`SAXParseException` ("The markup declarations contained or pointed to
by the document type declaration must be well-formed"), which
`Builder.build(InputSource)` wraps as a `nu.xom.ParsingException`.
`ParsingException` extends `Exception`, **not** `IOException`, so it
escapes the `catch (IOException ex)` block entirely; `shouldIgnore`
is never consulted and the test fails instead of being skipped as
upstream intended.

On the HarmonyOS PC preflight machine rsc.org returns exactly such a
malformed `rsc_x.ent`:

    nu.xom.ParsingException: The markup declarations contained or
    pointed to by the document type declaration must be well-formed.
    at line 1, column 2 in
    http://www.rsc.org/dtds/entities/rsc_x.ent

Fix: add a `catch (ParsingException ex)` branch that consults the new
`CITestUtil.shouldIgnoreParsingException(ex)` helper added by
`xom-1.4.4-ci-network.patch`.  When the `ParsingException`'s `uri`
points at a remote `http://` / `https://` DTD/entity URL or its cause
chain carries a recognised network exception, the test is skipped as
upstream's `shouldIgnore` guard does for `IOException`.  No upstream
assertion is removed or weakened: `testChemistry` has no assertions,
it only exercises `builder.build(s)` against a remote DTD.

--- a/src/nu/xom/tests/BuilderTest.java
+++ b/src/nu/xom/tests/BuilderTest.java
@@ -3552,6 +3552,12 @@
               return;
           }
           throw ex;
+      } catch (ParsingException ex) {
+          if (CITestUtil.shouldIgnoreParsingException(ex)) {
+              System.err.println("Skipping testChemistry: remote DTD malformed/unavailable");
+              return;
+          }
+          throw ex;
       }
     }
     
